{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nRebar's CLI facade on current main is 714 lines and reconstructs command identity and policy through 19 overlapping frozensets, an intercept ladder, package-help filenames, a regex-based reference generator, and repeated test censuses. The existing CLI reference is generated, but its syntax authority is not: it embeds 54 hand-maintained help resources plus a curated intercept table, so parser/help drift can pass while the census remains complete.\n\nTwenty-one intercept-only commands lack safe canonical help. `rebar help <command>` reports them unknown, while handler-owned `--help` is inconsistent and can execute work. The maintainer approved normalizing every visible command and approved deriving syntax/options from the same parser definitions used by execution.\n\n## Approved Architecture\nCreate one stdlib-only immutable lazy route registry. A route records canonical identity, spellings/visibility, lazy handler and parser-factory references, a bounded invocation-adapter kind, generated package-help key, mount/init policy, confirmation/output policy, and possible semantic capabilities. The registry is a routing table, not a plugin system, DI container, or middleware framework.\n\nEvery command grammar is built by a side-effect-free stdlib `argparse` factory. Runtime handlers and build-time generation call that same factory. Existing argparse builders are extracted; imperative parsers migrate with accepted/rejected argv, `--` handling, diagnostics, streams, and exit codes pinned by behavioral tests. A narrow shared ArgumentParser subclass/formatter may preserve Rebar diagnostics; no docs-only parser, source-regex grammar, Click dependency, or second parser is introduced.\n\nGeneration resolves all parser factories at build/check time using fixed `prog`, width, and formatter policy, then commits package-help and overview bytes. Runtime overview/help, aliases, malformed global syntax, and unknown-command output read only those bytes before RP-04 operation snapshot, config materialization, mount, handler resolution, or optional imports. The lexical top-level selector identifies only the spelling and first-token help/unknown forms; nested argv remains selected-handler-owned.\n\nA separate semantic capability registry maps capabilities to packaging extras and missing postures: `error`, `unavailable`, `abstain`, or intentional `fallback`. Routes advertise possibilities only; checks occur after the selected mode/backend is known, and domain components retain degraded result shapes.\n\n## Compatibility Contracts\nExact aliases reuse canonical grammar/help. `bridge-fsck`, `bridge-probe`, and `jira-onboard` compose canonical argument-definition helpers with alias-specific `prog`/replacement prose; `jira-onboard` keeps its historical spelling. Hidden `bridge-status` is explicitly helpable but undiscoverable. `purge-bridge` remains unknown.\n\nHelp/unknown paths never compose an operation or mount. Execution retains central best-effort mount, strict init, confirmation/global-output behavior, legacy JSON shapes, tokens after `--`, and established streams/exit codes. Accepted grammar changes are limited to the separately approved safe-help normalization unless a ticket names another intentional delta. `rebar-mcp` remains a separate entry point.\n\n## Migration and Rollback\nUse expand/contract by command family. Add the registry/parser contract and derived compatibility exports; migrate each handler and its runtime parser together; generate authoritative artifacts only after the complete parser/route census passes; then move execution, capabilities, docs/distribution consumers, and finally remove compatibility authorities. No shipped phase may contain a docs-only grammar runtime ignores.\n\nDuring expansion, rollback selects derived compatibility exports and reverts a parser family with its generated artifacts. After contraction, code revert restores the old router. Ticket, event, bridge, and binding formats do not change.\n\n## Delivery Order\n1. Record the architecture decision and prior-art constraints.\n2. Establish the parser/registry foundation; migrate core and advanced parser families in parallel; generate canonical package help only after both complete.\n3. Migrate lazy execution and semantic capability boundaries.\n4. Derive reference/distribution checks from registries and generated parser artifacts.\n5. Retire duplicate authorities and complete cutover.\n\n## Testing\nUse unit tests for pure registry/parser validation; command-family production-path parity tests for accepted/rejected argv and errors; real subprocess tests for exact committed bytes, streams, exit codes, aliases, and absence of operation/config/mount/handler effects; a clean no-extras wheel for every help form; and a full-extra lane for lazy-reference resolution. Generator `--check` must prove parser-rendered artifacts and `docs/cli-reference.md` are byte-current.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Every command's runtime parsing and generated syntax/options come from one side-effect-free argparse factory; no docs-only/manual syntax authority remains.\n- [ ] Every visible canonical command appears once in the grouped overview and both top-level help forms return byte-current generated package data before operation/config/store/handler boundaries.\n- [ ] Exact, compatibility, hidden, and retired spellings exhibit the approved behavior, including historical `jira-onboard` and unknown `purge-bridge`.\n- [ ] Lazy execution preserves mount/init, argv, confirmation, stream, exit-code, and legacy-output contracts except the explicit help normalization.\n- [ ] Selected optional modes/backends preserve approved capability postures without eager optional imports.\n- [ ] CLI-reference generation, coverage, lean-wheel checks, and full-extra resolution enumerate registries/parser artifacts rather than source regexes or manual command censuses.\n- [ ] Duplicate authorities are removed after shadow parity; quality gates pass and no stored-data migration occurs.\n- [ ] ADR 0099 and living architecture record the parser, route, capability, migration, compatibility, and rollback boundaries."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787070377508818001,"uuid":"72059be4-b8e2-4841-a325-71aa1b089647"}